   {
    "text": "Adulthood was a time of quiet rebellion. The world had changed, and so had the people in it. My cousins, the ones I’d always thought were just cousins, had grown up in a different way. They weren’t the same as I was. They had their own lives, their own dreams, and their own ways of seeing the world.\n\nI used to think that adulthood was about growing up, about becoming someone else. But the truth was, it was about becoming yourself. My cousins, though, had a different kind of growth. They were the ones who didn’t wait for the world to change before they changed too. They were the ones who took risks, who made choices, who lived with the consequences.\n\nOne of my cousins, Lila, had left home to study abroad. She didn’t come back for years. I missed her, but I also respected her. She had a way of seeing things that I hadn’t. She was the kind of person who didn’t just live in the present; she lived in the future. And that was something I admired.\n\nAnother cousin, Marcus, had become a teacher. He taught kids who didn’t have the same opportunities I did. He was the kind of person who made a difference, who gave others a chance. I admired him too, even if I didn’t always understand his choices.\n\nMy cousins were the ones who showed me what adulthood was really about. They weren’t just family; they were a part of me. They were the ones who made me see that adulthood wasn’t about being perfect or being ready. It was about being true to yourself, even when the world didn’t understand.\n\nAnd in the end, I realized that adulthood wasn’t a destination. It was a journey, and my cousins were the ones who helped me walk it. They were the ones who showed me that even in the quiet moments, there was always something to learn.",
